honest_ceiling:
On a fanless 8-core M2 Air (16 GB), a 5 km free-roam world at a locked 30 fps (warm, about 0.67 render scale with MetalFX Temporal to 1280x800 or above) can realistically reach lighting parity with UE5.8 frames g–j within about 150–200 m of the camera. That covers dappled sunflecks, deep sky-occluded shade with bright sky gaps, haze and eye adaptation. The Godot SDFGI prototype already meets the exposure-key, sunfleck and black-fraction targets headless. Warm highlights, visible shafts and brighter sky gaps are still tuning work.

The world can also beat UE5.8 overall where UE5.8's flaws were content, not engine:
- a physical sky with proper exposure instead of a clipped white sky;
- far canopy coloured from the orthophoto and shaded from the measured CHM instead of dark flat masses;
- measured 0.5 m terrain with triplanar and height-blended materials instead of stretching and blotches;
- opaque-core foliage instead of dithered stipple;
- a denser understorey and litter.

My judgement is about 80–90% of the Lumen/VSM lighting look. It will not match Lumen and VSM on:
- multi-bounce and off-screen bounce;
- sharp canopy shadows hundreds of metres out (the baked CHM term is blurrier);
- time-of-day changes without re-baking;
- Nanite-level mid-distance density.

It will not be truly photoreal: trees are the ceiling, because no scan-quality Tsuga sieboldii or Chamaecyparis exists and EZ-Tree gives billboard foliage.

Going genuinely beyond UE5.8 needs three things, most important first:
1. Species-accurate scanned assets: paid Megascans bark, needle cards and ground, and/or the owner's own full-resolution photogrammetry of rocks and roots.
2. A fan-cooled M3-or-later Mac for sustained clocks, hardware RT, MetalFX denoised upscaling and more bandwidth, or a Windows/RTX machine. Either enables heavier real-time GI; the Mac mini M4 at about £599 is from memory and unverified.
3. For non-interactive stills and fly-throughs only, Cycles path tracing via bpy, which can exceed UE5.8 today.
